course: world
lesson: 6
title: Gravity
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Gravity is a pull that every object has on every other object; the more matter (mass) something has, the stronger it pulls.
explain: The Earth is so massive that its pull holds us on the ground and makes a dropped apple fall towards its centre, which is why «down» points to the middle of the Earth everywhere, even in Australia.
explain: The Moon is falling towards the Earth all the time, but it also moves sideways so fast that it keeps missing: like a ball whirled on a string, it goes round and round, with gravity as the string; if the string snapped, the ball would fly off in a straight line.
explain: In the same way the Sun's gravity keeps the Earth and the other planets going around it.
explain: Without air, a feather and a hammer fall equally fast: an astronaut tried it on the Moon, and both reached the ground together.
fact: Gravity is a pull between all objects that have mass.
fact: The more mass an object has, the stronger its gravity.
fact: Gravity gets weaker the farther apart two objects are.
fact: The Earth's gravity pulls everything towards the centre of the Earth.
fact: Weight is the pull of gravity on an object.
fact: The Earth's gravity keeps the Moon going around the Earth.
fact: The Sun's gravity keeps the planets going around the Sun.
fact: Without air, light and heavy objects fall at the same speed.
fact: A feather falls slowly on the Earth because the air pushes against it.
fact: Even in air, two small solid balls, one heavy and one light, dropped from a low height land almost together, because the air hardly slows them.
fact: Gravity also holds the air and the oceans to the Earth.
fact: The Moon's gravity pulls on the oceans and helps make the tides, the sea rising and falling along the shore about twice a day.
fact: Gravity works in space too; astronauts float because they are falling around the Earth together with their ship.
exercise: What is gravity? | A pull between all objects that have mass | A push that only magnets make | A force that only works on the Earth and nowhere else | A wind that blows things down
exercise: Why does a dropped apple fall to the ground? | The Earth's gravity pulls it | The air pushes it down from above | Heavy things like to go down | The Sun pushes it away
exercise: Which way is «down» everywhere on the Earth? | Towards the centre of the Earth | Towards the South Pole | Towards the North Pole | Towards the Sun
exercise: Why does the Moon go around the Earth instead of flying away? | The Earth's gravity keeps pulling it | It is tied to the Earth by magnetism | The Sun pushes it towards the Earth | It is stuck in the Earth's air
exercise: Why does the Moon not fall onto the Earth? | It moves sideways so fast that it keeps missing the Earth | There is no gravity at all up in space, so it just stays there | The Moon is lighter than air | The Sun's light holds it up
exercise: What keeps the planets going around the Sun? | The Sun's gravity | The Sun's light pushing them | The Moon's gravity | Strong winds in space
exercise: What is weight? | The pull of gravity on an object | How big an object is | How much space an object takes up | How hot an object is
exercise: Which has the stronger gravity, the Earth or a football? | The Earth, because it has much more mass | The football, because it is smaller and closer to us | They have the same gravity | Neither, only magnets can pull
exercise: Why does a feather fall slower than a stone on the Earth? | The air pushes against it more | Gravity does not pull feathers | Feathers have no weight at all | The feather is pulled up by the Sun
exercise: What happened when an astronaut dropped a hammer and a feather on the Moon? | They hit the ground together | The hammer fell much faster | The feather floated away | Neither of them fell
exercise: What happens to gravity as objects get farther apart? | It gets weaker | It gets stronger | It stays the same | It turns into a push
exercise: What do the Moon's gravity and the oceans make together? | The tides | The waves of storms | The colour of the sea | The salt in the water
exercise: Why do astronauts in a spaceship float? | They and the ship are falling around the Earth together | There is no gravity at all in space, so nothing can pull them | Their suits are full of light gas | The Sun pulls them upward
exercise: What holds the air around the Earth? | The Earth's gravity | The clouds | The Moon's light | A glass shell around the sky
test: A child in Australia drops a ball. Which way does it fall? | Towards the ground, towards the Earth's centre | Up into the sky | Sideways, towards the equator in the middle of the Earth | Towards Europe
test: If the Earth's gravity suddenly switched off, what would the Moon do? | Fly off in a straight line | Fall onto the Earth | Stop and hang still | Go around the Earth faster
test: Which is true? | Heavy and light things fall equally fast when there is no air | Heavy things always fall faster, even when there is no air at all | Light things fall faster than heavy ones | Nothing falls where there is no air
test: Leo whirls a ball on a string around his head. What plays the part of the string for the Moon? | The Earth's gravity | The Sun's light | The Earth's air | The Moon's speed
test: A ball is thrown sideways. Why does it curve down to the ground? | Gravity pulls it down while it moves sideways | The air turns it downwards | It gets tired of moving | The ground pulls it down with a kind of magnetism
test: Why does the Earth go around the Sun and not around the Moon? | The Sun has far more mass, so its pull is far stronger | The Moon has no gravity at all | The Sun is nearer to the Earth than the Moon, so it pulls harder | The Moon's light pushes the Earth away
test: What would happen to the oceans if the Earth had no gravity? | They would drift off into space | They would freeze at once | They would flow to the poles | Nothing would happen to them
test: An astronaut floats inside the space station. Which is true? | Gravity still pulls her, but she is falling around the Earth | There is no gravity where she is | She has become weightless because she is so high above the ground | The station is held up by air
test: Two balls of the same size, one of steel and one of rubber, are dropped from a low wall. What happens? | They land almost together | The steel ball lands much sooner | The rubber ball lands much sooner | The rubber ball floats upward
test: why don't people at the bottom of the world fall off | Gravity pulls everyone towards the Earth's centre | They hold on because the ground is sticky | The air pushes them against the ground | They really are upside down and need belts to hold them on
test: drop a hammer n a feather on the moon, which lands first | Both land together, there's no air to slow the feather | The hammer, coz heavy things always fall faster than light ones | The feather, it floats down faster without air | Neither, nothing falls on the Moon
test: my brother says there's no gravity in space, that's why astronauts float. true? | No, they float because they're falling around the Earth with their ship | Yes, gravity stops where the air ends | Yes, space has no gravity at all | No, they float because their suits are filled with air to keep them up
test: Mia reckons heavy stuff always falls faster than light stuff. right? | No, without air they fall at the same speed | Yes, twice as heavy falls twice as fast | Yes, that's why a feather falls slowly | No, light things always fall faster
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the quantity designated as weight? | The force exerted by gravity upon an object | The quantity of matter an object contains | The volume an object occupies | The density of the material of an object
exam: Identify the principal agency by which the planets are retained in their orbits. | The gravitational attraction of the Sun | The pressure of the solar wind | The magnetism of the Earth | The friction of interplanetary air
exam: It is given that a hammer and a feather are released simultaneously in the absence of air. Determine the outcome. | Both descend at an identical rate | The hammer descends considerably more rapidly | The feather descends considerably more rapidly | Neither descends, gravity being absent
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning astronauts in orbit about the Earth. | They float because they and their vessel are falling about the Earth together | They float because gravity is wholly absent at such a distance from the Earth | They float because the vessel is lighter than air | They float because the Moon draws them upward
